\mt1 EDITOR’S INTRODUCTION
\ip This volume brings together two English translations that have long been read apart. The Old Testament is the Septuagint in the translation of Sir Lancelot Charles Lee Brenton, as printed in 1870 and as published online by eBible.org, with the corrections eBible has made to it. Brenton’s own preface, introductions, notes, and appendices are kept. The New Testament is the Authorized, or King James, Version in the text of the Cambridge Paragraph Bible of 1873, edited by F. H. A. Scrivener, likewise as published online by eBible.org, together with the Epistle Dedicatory and The Translators to the Reader. Scrivener’s own critical introduction and appendices were never part of the online text and are not printed here. Both translations are in the public domain outside the United Kingdom. Brenton’s is also in the public domain in the UK, where the right to print and publish the King James Version belongs to the Crown and is licensed only to certain publishers.
\ip Each testament opens with the front matter of its own translation: Brenton’s preface, introductions, and list of abbreviations come before Genesis, and the Epistle Dedicatory and The Translators to the Reader before Matthew. Brenton’s appendices and eBible’s corrections follow Revelation.
\ip The books of the Old Testament are arranged in the order of the Church of Greece. The books that English Bibles set apart as the Apocrypha are printed in their Greek places, so there is no separate Apocrypha section: the Prayer of Manasses follows 2 Chronicles, 4 Maccabees follows 3 Maccabees, and the additions to Esther and Daniel and Psalm 151 stand within the text. Brenton’s single book of Ezra and Nehemiah is divided into 2 Esdras and Nehemias, and Malachias 3:19–24 is numbered 4:1–6, again following Greek practice. Apart from these, the chapter and verse numbers are those of the translations themselves. A few books are renamed in the same spirit: Brenton’s Joshua is Jesus, the Son of Navi, and his four books of Kings are the four books of Kingdoms. Every book is also given a full title in the manner of the Cambridge Paragraph Bible, whose Genesis is “The First Book of Moses, Called Genesis,” and a short name for the running heads.
\ip The wording of both translations has not been altered. Brenton’s footnotes are printed, with a few slips in eBible’s text of them corrected; a matching “See” note is replaced by a classified cross-reference. The online text of the Cambridge New Testament has no notes, so the New Testament carries instead the marginal notes of the King James Bible of 1611 as transcribed by Calvin George: the translators’ other possible renderings, their literal glosses of the Greek, and their remarks on its readings. Their marginal cross-references are not included. Words that the translators supplied to complete the English sense, which many Bibles print in italics, are set here in ordinary type. The title pages, the section dividers, the table of contents, and the headings are this edition’s own, and the opinions expressed in the introductions and appendices are those of their original authors.
\ip A quotation link stands at the first verse of each quoting and each quoted passage, and names the complete passage at the other end. After each reference, a short gloss gives the judgment of Turpie (1868) on how the quotation’s wording stands to the Hebrew and the Septuagint, in his five classes: “Heb. and LXX” (his class A), the New Testament agrees with both; “Heb. against LXX” (B), it follows the Hebrew where the Septuagint departs from it; “neither, LXX = Heb.” (C), it follows neither, where the Septuagint renders the Hebrew correctly; “LXX against Heb.” (D), it follows the Septuagint where it departs from the Hebrew; and “neither, LXX ≠ Heb.” (E), it follows neither, where the two also differ. These are Turpie’s textual judgments, not a fresh comparison of the KJV and Brenton or an exact verse-to-verse wording match.
\ip A link names Brenton’s chapter and verse, which in places differ from the King James Bible’s. Brenton numbers the psalms as the Greek does, most of them one lower, and he counts a psalm’s title as its first verse or two, so that Psalm 33:13–17 is the King James Bible’s 34:12–16. From chapter 25 on, Jeremias arranges its chapters in another order, set out in Brenton’s table among the appendices, so that Jeremias 38:31–34 is the King James Bible’s 31:31–34. Where the Septuagint adds to a verse, Brenton may set the addition apart as a lettered verse, which the King James Bible lacks: the cheerful giver of 2 Corinthians 9:7 is found at Proverbs 22:8a. The table below gives the King James numbering of the psalms and of every other linked verse that is numbered differently, apart from those in the chapters of Jeremias that Brenton’s table covers.
